<p>This week&#8217;s spotlight shines on your Mac&#8217;s display and all the things you can do with it, whether that&#8217;s capturing screenshots in more sophisticated ways or turning that screen into a smart teleprompter that listens for your voice. And if neither of those apps grabs you, we&#8217;ve got ones that make use of the notch at the top of your MacBook display or replace the Dock running along the bottom.</p>
<p>We&#8217;re not all about screens, though. For anyone on the lookout for interesting iPhone apps, we&#8217;ve got an iOS entry that promises to help you get a grip on all those subscriptions you&#8217;ve got floating around.</p>
<p>Got an app you think belongs in a future edition of the App Report? We&#8217;d love to hear from you at <code>apps@sixcolors.com</code> with a brief description of the app. We won&#8217;t be able to write back — we get a lot of suggestions, you know — but we will keep the app in mind going forward.</p>

<h2>Snapline</h2>
<figure class="pull-right narrow"><img height="1120" sizes="(max-width: 1010px) 23vw, 173px" srcset="https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/snapline3-6c.png?ssl=1&amp;w=690&amp;quality=90 690w, https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/snapline3-6c.png?ssl=1&amp;w=1035&amp;quality=90 1035w, https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/snapline3-6c.png?ssl=1&amp;w=1380&amp;quality=90 1380w, https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/snapline3-6c.png?ssl=1&amp;w=2070&amp;quality=90 2070w, https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/snapline3-6c.png?ssl=1&amp;w=2760&amp;quality=90 2760w" width="320" data-recalc-dims="1" decoding="async" src="https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/snapline3-6c.png?ssl=1&#038;w=1380&#038;quality=90" alt="Screenshot of a scrolling capture tool reading &quot;2,693 pt tall, ≈ 4.5 screens, shown at 1:6,&quot; with Cancel and Done buttons. The long preview shows social posts of dogs in costumes, including a basset hound, a graduation gown, and sweaters." /><figcaption></figcaption></figure>
<p>Your Mac comes with some built-in screenshot tools — there&#8217;s even been a built-in Screenshot app since macOS Mojave. But for my money, they lack some of the finer controls when you have very exact capture needs. The <a href="https://snap-line.app">Snapline</a> utility makes those tools far more apparent and — more importantly — easy to use.</p>
<p>You essentially just have to remember three key combinations: Option-Command-S lets you capture an area of the screen that you specify, while Option-Command-L allows for rolling capture and Option-Command-R records your screen. When it&#8217;s time to review your screenshots, Control-Command-S shows your history, though you can also access any of these commands from Snapline&#8217;s place in your menu bar.</p>
<p>Using Snapline, I was able to grab a scrolling screenshot of a lengthy Bluesky thread featuring an assortment of angry pets wearing Halloween costumes that I then pulled from the app&#8217;s history and pasted into a Gmail message in my Safari browser. Annotation tools add to Snapline&#8217;s feature set if you need to do screenshot markup.</p>
<p>The app costs $25, though you can download it for free and see if it fits your needs with a 14-day trial.</p>
<h2>Tellie</h2>
<p>A while back, I included <a href="https://sixcolors.com/post/2026/09/app-report-the-apps-that-go-with-you/">Eyes Forward in an App Report</a>, and as much as I liked that macOS app, it&#8217;s not the only option if you&#8217;re looking for teleprompter-style software you can use for presentations, podcast ad reads or anything else that requires you to stay on script. <a href="https://tellieapp.com/">Tellie</a> is another macOS teleprompter, with this app relying on your voice to scroll through your script.</p>
<p>Tellie lives in your Mac&#8217;s menu bar and only pops out around the notch when you need to read a script. But the real magic happens when you allow Tellie to use your Mac&#8217;s microphone and enable speech recognition. That&#8217;s because the teleprompter follows along as you speak, highlighting words as you say them and scrolling at the pace you set. You can even improv and riff without losing your place — once you get back on script, Tellie resumes scrolling.</p>
<figure><img height="2070" sizes="(max-width: 1010px) calc(100vw - 60px), 690px" srcset="https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/tellie-6c.jpeg?ssl=1&amp;w=690&amp;quality=90 690w, https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/tellie-6c.jpeg?ssl=1&amp;w=1035&amp;quality=90 1035w, https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/tellie-6c.jpeg?ssl=1&amp;w=1380&amp;quality=90 1380w, https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/tellie-6c.jpeg?ssl=1&amp;w=2070&amp;quality=90 2070w, https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/tellie-6c.jpeg?ssl=1&amp;w=2760&amp;quality=90 2760w" width="2760" data-recalc-dims="1" decoding="async" src="https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/tellie-6c.jpeg?ssl=1&#038;w=1380&#038;quality=90" alt="Photo of a dusty laptop screen showing a teleprompter overlay reading a Big Hawaiian High-Rise Hotel ad with discount code SNELLSENTME, over The Incomparable's Magnum Podcast page hosted by Jason Snell, Philip Michaels, and David J. Loehr." /><figcaption></figcaption></figure>
<p>A Tellie feature I appreciate is the ability to set key phrases and important sections in your script. The text turns green the moment you cover the key points, which is a nice visual cue to help you move along in your presentation. And it&#8217;s one of several such touches built into Tellie.</p>
<p>You can download the app for a free 10-day trial. After that, the basic teleprompter features remain in place, but a $29 payment unlocks all of Tellie&#8217;s capabilities on up to three Macs.</p>
<h2>Altillo</h2>
<p>Add <a href="https://altillo.app">Altillo</a> to the ranks of apps that let you do more with the notch on a MacBook display. Like Droppy — a <a href="https://sixcolors.com/post/2026/10/app-report-apps-worth-grabbing/">similar app profiled</a> in this space — Altillo lets you drag files toward the top of your Mac screen where you can store them in a drop-down shelf that appears from out of the notch in your display. A Dynamic Island-esque counter shows how many files are accessible from up there.</p>
<figure><img height="528" sizes="(max-width: 1010px) calc(100vw - 60px), 690px" srcset="https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/altillo1-6c-1.png?ssl=1&amp;w=690&amp;quality=90 690w, https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/altillo1-6c-1.png?ssl=1&amp;w=1035&amp;quality=90 1035w, https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/altillo1-6c-1.png?ssl=1&amp;w=1380&amp;quality=90 1380w, https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/altillo1-6c-1.png?ssl=1&amp;w=2070&amp;quality=90 2070w, https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/altillo1-6c-1.png?ssl=1&amp;w=2760&amp;quality=90 2760w" width="1151" data-recalc-dims="1" decoding="async" src="https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/altillo1-6c-1.png?ssl=1&#038;w=1380&#038;quality=90" alt="A dark Mac shelf app window with the Shelf tab selected, showing three file thumbnails on a wood-grain surface: a text document, an illustration of a turtle chef eating pizza beside a man, and a form labeled DistributionForm. Header reads 3 and Empty." /><figcaption></figcaption></figure>
<p>But there&#8217;s more to Altillo&#8217;s feature set than that. As with similar apps, you can control music playback from the notch and access anything you&#8217;ve saved to your clipboard. But Altillo-specific features include a home assistant for managing connected smart devices as well as a monitor for your AI agent usage. Neither of those features really applies to what I use my Mac for, but if it&#8217;s the sort of activity you like to manage from one place, Altillo is worth checking out.</p>
<p>Another reason to give Altillo a download? It&#8217;s also free.</p>
<h2>Tungsten Edge</h2>
<p>For some people, the Mac&#8217;s Dock is the only way to navigate to different apps and open files. But if you&#8217;ve got an app with multiple windows open, wouldn&#8217;t it be better to jump directly to the window you want?</p>
<figure><img height="188" sizes="(max-width: 1010px) calc(100vw - 60px), 690px" srcset="https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/tungstenedge-6c.png?ssl=1&amp;w=690&amp;quality=90 690w, https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/tungstenedge-6c.png?ssl=1&amp;w=1035&amp;quality=90 1035w, https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/tungstenedge-6c.png?ssl=1&amp;w=1380&amp;quality=90 1380w, https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/tungstenedge-6c.png?ssl=1&amp;w=2070&amp;quality=90 2070w, https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/tungstenedge-6c.png?ssl=1&amp;w=2760&amp;quality=90 2760w" width="2248" data-recalc-dims="1" decoding="async" src="https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/tungstenedge-6c.png?ssl=1&#038;w=1380&#038;quality=90" alt="A macOS Dock showing app icons for Finder, Launchpad, Messages, BBEdit, System Settings, and News, plus minimized windows titled &quot;Homepage | Defector,&quot; &quot;Six Colors – Apple, tec...,&quot; &quot;libbib1.jpeg,&quot; and &quot;aaron-gnocchi.jpeg,&quot; with Downloads and Trash." /><figcaption></figcaption></figure>
<p>That seems to be the thought process behind <a href="https://tungstenedge.app/en/">Tungsten Edge</a>, a macOS utility that replaces the Dock with everything — and I do mean <em>everything</em> you have open at the moment. I use the Acorn 2 image editor, and if I&#8217;ve got multiple files open, all of them are visible (and clickable) in Tungsten Edge. The same goes for multiple windows in Safari, though tabs aren&#8217;t broken out individually.</p>
<p>The left side of the Tungsten Edge bar features a shelf where you can drag files for easy access. On the right, you&#8217;ll find a drawer where you can place frequently used apps. Tungsten Edge also offers a garbage can and quick access to your downloads.</p>
<p>The app is a free download on GitHub until it reaches its 1.0 version. (I downloaded version 0.18.4 to test Tungsten Edge.) After that, it will be $24, though if you register as a founding user on the Tungsten Edge website, you&#8217;ll get a lifetime license.</p>
<h2>Duefolio</h2>
<figure class="pull-right narrow"><img height="2760" sizes="(max-width: 1010px) 23vw, 173px" srcset="https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/duefolio-6c.png?ssl=1&amp;w=690&amp;quality=90 690w, https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/duefolio-6c.png?ssl=1&amp;w=1035&amp;quality=90 1035w, https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/duefolio-6c.png?ssl=1&amp;w=1380&amp;quality=90 1380w, https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/duefolio-6c.png?ssl=1&amp;w=2070&amp;quality=90 2070w, https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/duefolio-6c.png?ssl=1&amp;w=2760&amp;quality=90 2760w" width="1350" data-recalc-dims="1" decoding="async" src="https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/duefolio-6c.png?ssl=1&#038;w=1380&#038;quality=90" alt="iPhone screenshot of a subscription tracker. Where it goes: Phone Bill $197/mo (87%), Apple TV+ $18/mo (8%), Apple Music $12/mo (5%), all upcoming. Renewals: Nov 03 $197, Nov 13 $18, Nov 27 $12. 30-day total $227.00. A composition grid and Pro badge appear below." /><figcaption></figcaption></figure>
<p>For years, I&#8217;ve been suggesting a built-in iOS app that tracks my subscriptions. To be fair, your iPhone does track the subscriptions you purchase via Apple — launch Settings, tap your name and then select Subscriptions to see the apps and services you&#8217;re on the hook for — but I had something a little bit broader in mind where you could enter other recurring subscriptions.</p>
<p><a href="https://apps.apple.com/us/app/duefolio-manage-subscriptions/id6795091979">Duefolio</a> currently comes the closest to providing what I&#8217;m looking for. This iPhone app shows all the subscriptions you enter on a timeline, so that you can better visualize when each one comes due. Even better, a Financials tab in the app calculates your monthly and annual spending on subscriptions, which can be important data for helping you shed costly recurring items from your budget.</p>
<p>There are a few interface quirks that would take a little getting used to. For instance, when you enter a subscription, Duefolio requires an end date — something that makes no sense if it&#8217;s a recurring charge. An auto-renew toggle ensures that your charge repeats every month, but that makes the Billing End field feel even more unnecessary.</p>
<p>Duefolio comes with a built-in list of subscriptions you can add for free — everything from Apple TV to AI subscriptions like Perplexity and Claude. If you want to add custom subscriptions, though, you&#8217;ll need to pay a one-time $15 fee, which nets you other features like home screen widgets and deeper analysis tools.</p>
